1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,480
 Hey everyone, Ryan here, and welcome back to our oral medicine series.

2
00:00:11,480 --> 00:00:15,720
 This video will be all about diabetes.

3
00:00:15,720 --> 00:00:18,560
 So we're going to start with diabetes mellitus.

4
00:00:18,560 --> 00:00:24,200
 And mellitus comes from the Latin, meaning sweetened with honey, which refers to the

5
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:28,000
 presence of sugar in the urine.

6
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:32,940
 Now glucose is the main sugar that we use for energy as humans.

7
00:00:32,940 --> 00:00:38,800
 And diabetes mellitus leads to an excess of glucose in the blood.

8
00:00:38,800 --> 00:00:43,480
 High blood glucose is called hyperglycemia.

9
00:00:43,480 --> 00:00:49,880
 Now insulin is a hormone naturally produced by beta cells of the pancreas.

10
00:00:49,880 --> 00:00:56,800
 And insulin's job is to capture glucose in the blood and transport it into cells where

11
00:00:56,800 --> 00:01:00,520
 it can be used for energy, which is a good thing.

12
00:01:00,520 --> 00:01:07,080
 But if insulin doesn't function properly, or it just isn't produced, then glucose is

13
00:01:07,080 --> 00:01:12,680
 stuck in the blood where it builds up and that can actually be a bad thing.

14
00:01:12,680 --> 00:01:18,640
 Now glucose is a high energy reactive molecule and it'll actually cause oxidative damage

15
00:01:18,640 --> 00:01:22,320
 to the endothelial cells that lie in the blood vessels.

16
00:01:22,320 --> 00:01:28,880
 And damage to arteries can lead to atherosclerosis, which can in turn lead to hypertension, heart

17
00:01:28,880 --> 00:01:33,120
 attacks, stroke, and peripheral vascular disease.

18
00:01:33,120 --> 00:01:42,160
 And damage to capillaries can cause neuropathy, aka diabetic nerve pain, retinopathy and nephropathy.

19
00:01:42,160 --> 00:01:48,400
 So some bad things about having high glucose around in the blood.

20
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:55,160
 Now some of the most classic signs and symptoms of diabetes mellitus include polydipsia, polyphagia,

21
00:01:55,160 --> 00:01:56,720
 and polyurea.

22
00:01:56,720 --> 00:02:02,480
 So this means that you're very thirsty, you're very hungry, and you urinate excessively.

23
00:02:02,480 --> 00:02:09,400
 So the saying goes you eat a lot, drink a lot, and pee a lot.

24
00:02:09,400 --> 00:02:14,960
 Now as a side note, there are also other mechanisms of developing diabetes such as diabetes

25
00:02:14,960 --> 00:02:24,200
 insipidus, and insipidus, instead of mellitus this time, refers to the word insipid, which

26
00:02:24,200 --> 00:02:30,480
 in turn is with regards to the passing of a lot of bland, colorless urine, instead of

27
00:02:30,480 --> 00:02:34,000
 the sweet sugary urine and diabetes mellitus.

28
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:39,200
 So it's a condition where the kidneys are unable to retain water and this actually has

29
00:02:39,200 --> 00:02:42,960
 nothing to do with insulin or blood glucose.

30
00:02:42,960 --> 00:02:49,000
 So you just produce large amounts of urine because of a pituitary gland deficiency or

31
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:51,560
 an insensitivity to ADH.

32
00:02:51,560 --> 00:03:01,080
 Now ADH, also known as vasopressin, stands for anti-diuretic hormone, and a lack of anti-diuretic

33
00:03:01,080 --> 00:03:05,280
 hormone will lead to diuresis or peeing.

34
00:03:05,280 --> 00:03:10,520
 So you're constantly thirsty, even if you have something to drink because you're constantly

35
00:03:10,520 --> 00:03:12,440
 peeing it out.

36
00:03:12,440 --> 00:03:18,000
 Diabetes isn't really an element for diabetes insipidus, it's all about the liquids here.

37
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:26,240
 So you typically would treat this with a synthetic ADH, a low salt diet, and/or IV fluids.

38
00:03:26,240 --> 00:03:30,200
 But for the rest of the video though, we're going to shift back our focus to the much

39
00:03:30,200 --> 00:03:34,960
 more common diabetes mellitus.

40
00:03:34,960 --> 00:03:42,920
 So for type 1 diabetes mellitus, 5 to 10% of your diabetes cases are going to be type 1.

41
00:03:42,920 --> 00:03:48,000
 It usually has a juvenile onset, which means it usually affects children.

42
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:54,400
 It's insulin dependent, which means that this diabetes depends entirely on a lack of insulin

43
00:03:54,400 --> 00:03:55,720
 production.

44
00:03:55,720 --> 00:04:02,720
 So usually some autoimmune reaction kills those beta cells, which usually produce insulin.

45
00:04:02,720 --> 00:04:07,480
 So insulin's not being produced naturally by the body, and these patients have to take

46
00:04:07,480 --> 00:04:10,160
 insulin synthetically.

47
00:04:10,160 --> 00:04:14,520
 Of course there's an insulin deficiency, as explained above, the body does not make

48
00:04:14,520 --> 00:04:16,200
 enough insulin.

49
00:04:16,200 --> 00:04:21,880
 And without insulin, there's no sugar inside these cells that desperately need energy.

50
00:04:21,880 --> 00:04:28,720
 So in the absence of sugar, cells are burning lipids, which produces toxic ketone bodies.

51
00:04:28,720 --> 00:04:35,280
 This is called ketosis if it's more mild, or ketoacidosis if it's more severe.

52
00:04:35,280 --> 00:04:39,880
 And this can result in a fruity smelling ketone breath.

53
00:04:39,880 --> 00:04:44,800
 There's no way to prevent this, and by this I mean there's a strong genetic component

54
00:04:44,800 --> 00:04:45,800
 to type 1 diabetes.

55
00:04:45,800 --> 00:04:50,120
 You can't blame diet or exercise here.

56
00:04:50,120 --> 00:04:56,000
 In all cases, if they're going to be managed properly, require insulin injections.

57
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:01,260
 Now let's compare this slide to the next one, Type 2 Diabetes.

58
00:05:01,260 --> 00:05:07,760
 This one accounts for 85 to 90% of all diabetes cases, and it's one of the most common chronic

59
00:05:07,760 --> 00:05:09,800
 diseases out there.

60
00:05:09,800 --> 00:05:15,440
 It usually has an adult onset, which means it usually is the result of obesity, poor

61
00:05:15,440 --> 00:05:19,600
 diet, lack of physical activity, things like that.

62
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:25,040
 It's non-insulin dependent, in other words it's more complicated, so there may be a component

63
00:05:25,040 --> 00:05:31,240
 of less insulin production, but more importantly, even if insulin's floating around in the blood,

64
00:05:31,240 --> 00:05:38,600
 the target cells are no longer sensitive to insulin, so it can't do its job.

65
00:05:38,600 --> 00:05:40,600
 So this is called insulin resistance.

66
00:05:40,600 --> 00:05:46,720
 The body can't use insulin properly, it's resistant to insulin's effects.

67
00:05:46,720 --> 00:05:52,760
 Most cases can be prevented, again usually the cause of obesity, poor diet, lack of physical

68
00:05:52,760 --> 00:05:58,120
 activity, and late stage cases may require insulin injections.

69
00:05:58,120 --> 00:06:05,080
 So early on, insulin really does you no good, but later on the pancreatic islets get burnt

70
00:06:05,080 --> 00:06:10,360
 out trying to produce more insulin all the time, and then you actually might need to

71
00:06:10,360 --> 00:06:15,320
 have synthetic insulin injections to try to help you out.

72
00:06:15,320 --> 00:06:21,280
 A third type of diabetes mellitus is called gestational diabetes, which occurs in pregnant

73
00:06:21,280 --> 00:06:28,080
 women who otherwise don't have diabetes, and it's due to placental hormones, and it's basically

74
00:06:28,080 --> 00:06:37,560
 a combination of type 1 with insulin deficiency and type 2 with insulin resistance.

75
00:06:37,560 --> 00:06:44,640
 So this usually resolves though after the baby is delivered.

76
00:06:44,640 --> 00:06:50,880
 So there are two main ways to measure diabetes, and the first one is blood glucose measured

77
00:06:50,880 --> 00:06:52,880
 with a glucometer.

78
00:06:52,880 --> 00:06:59,600
 This is the relatively easy way, and how it works is you wash your hands, you prick your

79
00:06:59,600 --> 00:07:07,480
 finger, and you place a drop of blood into this test strip that's inserted in the glucometer.

80
00:07:07,480 --> 00:07:13,400
 And the device then reads out your blood sugar in milligrams per deciliter.

81
00:07:13,400 --> 00:07:19,080
 So while it's relatively easy to measure, it's not reliable because blood glucose goes

82
00:07:19,080 --> 00:07:24,160
 up and down throughout the day, depending on when you eat and last, what you eat, and

83
00:07:24,160 --> 00:07:27,320
 when you took the measurement.

84
00:07:27,320 --> 00:07:34,040
 So we'll go over what a good measurement looks like in the next slide on classifications.

85
00:07:34,040 --> 00:07:40,960
 Now the second test we have is the HBA1C test, and this is a much more valuable test, and

86
00:07:40,960 --> 00:07:43,680
 it's also asked more on the board exam.

87
00:07:43,680 --> 00:07:52,600
 So this one stands for hemoglobin A1C, which is a glycated or glycosylated form of hemoglobin.

88
00:07:52,600 --> 00:07:58,760
 It's basically hemoglobin, which is that protein in red blood cells that transports oxygen

89
00:07:58,760 --> 00:08:01,640
 that's coated with sugar.

90
00:08:01,640 --> 00:08:09,520
 And importantly, this modification is permanent for the entire lifespan of that red blood

91
00:08:09,520 --> 00:08:14,760
 cell, which is about 120 days.

92
00:08:14,760 --> 00:08:21,120
 So a sample of blood will be taken, usually from your arm, and this test will tell you

93
00:08:21,120 --> 00:08:27,200
 the percentage of hemoglobin proteins in your blood sample that are coated with sugar.

94
00:08:27,200 --> 00:08:31,920
 And this gives you your blood glucose level over the past three months, because again,

95
00:08:31,920 --> 00:08:35,440
 that's the average lifespan of a red blood cell.

96
00:08:35,440 --> 00:08:42,720
 And the higher your A1C percent level is, the worse your blood sugar control and the

97
00:08:42,720 --> 00:08:47,680
 higher your risk of diabetes complications.

98
00:08:47,680 --> 00:08:51,000
 All right, now on to the diabetes categories.

99
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:55,080
 And this is straight from the American Diabetes Association.

100
00:08:55,080 --> 00:08:56,880
 So there are three rows.

101
00:08:56,880 --> 00:09:03,720
 We have normal or healthy, we have prediabetes, which is the equivalent of pre-hypertension

102
00:09:03,720 --> 00:09:10,440
 or elevated blood pressure, it's not quite a disease state, but it is higher than normal.

103
00:09:10,440 --> 00:09:15,360
 And then we have diabetes, which is the equivalent of hypertension, but for blood sugar.

104
00:09:15,360 --> 00:09:20,520
 So hypertension is for blood pressure, diabetes is for blood sugar.

105
00:09:20,520 --> 00:09:25,800
 And the two often go hand in hand, it's what we call co-morbidities.

106
00:09:25,800 --> 00:09:29,320
 They tend to happen together, but not always.

107
00:09:29,320 --> 00:09:37,800
 And there are also three columns, we have the HBA1C, the fasting plasma glucose test,

108
00:09:37,800 --> 00:09:41,360
 and the oral glucose tolerance test.

109
00:09:41,360 --> 00:09:47,760
 And remember, as dentists, we're not diagnosing people with diabetes, we're simply evaluating

110
00:09:47,760 --> 00:09:54,200
 their current health status, and then we either modify our dental treatment and/or refer them

111
00:09:54,200 --> 00:10:01,180
 to a physician for further evaluation, diagnosis, and treatment when necessary.

112
00:10:01,180 --> 00:10:07,040
 So the HBA1C test is what we just talked about, it's taking a sample of red blood cells and

113
00:10:07,040 --> 00:10:11,080
 measuring the percentage of hemoglobin that's coated with sugar.

114
00:10:11,080 --> 00:10:15,040
 And this gives you a reliable look at the blood glucose situation for the last two to

115
00:10:15,040 --> 00:10:16,400
 three months.

116
00:10:16,400 --> 00:10:22,520
 And this is used by doctors to diagnose someone with diabetes, but for someone already with

117
00:10:22,520 --> 00:10:28,640
 diabetes, we want them to be ideally under 7%.

118
00:10:28,640 --> 00:10:35,360
 We want them to be under 7% for a relatively well-controlled situation.

119
00:10:35,360 --> 00:10:43,840
 These numbers here, 6.5% or above, 5.7 to 6.4 and below 5.7, are more used for diagnosis.

120
00:10:43,840 --> 00:10:50,240
 Again, if someone already has diabetes, we're looking for under 7% for a well-controlled

121
00:10:50,240 --> 00:10:51,240
 situation.

122
00:10:51,240 --> 00:10:59,320
 So, as dentists, we can ask a patient what their last HBA1C reading was to get an idea,

123
00:10:59,320 --> 00:11:03,760
 are they well-controlled at less than 7% or are they poorly controlled, which would be

124
00:11:03,760 --> 00:11:10,960
 above 8%, and that might inform how we proceed with treatment.

125
00:11:10,960 --> 00:11:18,600
 And of course, we want that HBA1C measurement to have been taken within the last three months.

126
00:11:18,600 --> 00:11:23,360
 Then we have the fasting plasma glucose test.

127
00:11:23,360 --> 00:11:29,600
 And fasting is defined as not having anything to eat or drink except water for at least eight

128
00:11:29,600 --> 00:11:32,800
 hours before the test.

129
00:11:32,800 --> 00:11:41,080
 And finally, the oral glucose tolerance test is technically pretty complicated, but basically

130
00:11:41,080 --> 00:11:45,880
 it involves you fasting for eight hours, and then you drink this really sweet drink and

131
00:11:45,880 --> 00:11:52,520
 then check your blood glucose two hours after that to reveal how your body processes glucose.

132
00:11:52,520 --> 00:11:58,960
 Alternatively, it can just be taken approximately two hours post-prandial, which means after

133
00:11:58,960 --> 00:12:01,120
 having a meal.

134
00:12:01,120 --> 00:12:06,240
 So, for fasting, we want to be below 100.

135
00:12:06,240 --> 00:12:09,080
 We want to be 99 or below.

136
00:12:09,080 --> 00:12:14,000
 But not too low, as we'll see in a little bit, too low can actually be a bad thing.

137
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:21,680
 And then for post-prandial, we want to be below 140 or 139 and below.

138
00:12:21,680 --> 00:12:28,960
 For the pre-diabetes rows, these orange values are given different names depending on what

139
00:12:28,960 --> 00:12:30,720
 test was used.

140
00:12:30,720 --> 00:12:38,400
 So if you score between 100 and 125 with the fasting glucose test, it's called impaired

141
00:12:38,400 --> 00:12:40,360
 fasting glucose.

142
00:12:40,360 --> 00:12:44,800
 IFG would be what you would call that.

143
00:12:44,800 --> 00:12:51,760
 If you score between 140 and 199 with the glucose tolerance test, it's called impaired

144
00:12:51,760 --> 00:12:56,000
 glucose tolerance, or IGT.

145
00:12:56,000 --> 00:13:01,320
 And then findings in the red row would point to a diagnosis of diabetes.

146
00:13:01,320 --> 00:13:10,000
 Okay, so we talked about measuring, we talked about categories, now how do we manage diabetes?

147
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:13,440
 Well for Type 1, it's all about getting that insulin.

148
00:13:13,440 --> 00:13:18,240
 And so you're going to treat it with self-administered, subcutaneous insulin.

149
00:13:18,240 --> 00:13:23,000
 And these are all synthetic derivatives of that hormone.

150
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:28,440
 For this graph, the y-axis is intensity of the effect of insulin.

151
00:13:28,440 --> 00:13:32,480
 The x-axis is duration in hours.

152
00:13:32,480 --> 00:13:38,280
 So we'll start with the human insulin as a baseline.

153
00:13:38,280 --> 00:13:43,000
 Human insulin has a strong and relatively rapid effect.

154
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:50,480
 For the synthetic forms, lispro, aspart and gluysine are rapid-acting forms.

155
00:13:50,480 --> 00:13:55,600
 They're stronger and faster-acting than the human insulin, but their effect dies down

156
00:13:55,600 --> 00:13:57,600
 pretty quick.

157
00:13:57,600 --> 00:14:04,880
 NPH, or neutral prodomine h-dorn, is a weaker and longer-acting form.

158
00:14:04,880 --> 00:14:11,720
 And then Glargine and Detimer are even weaker and longer-acting than that.

159
00:14:11,720 --> 00:14:19,200
 So a common prescription would be to combine one rapid-acting form of insulin right before

160
00:14:19,200 --> 00:14:26,760
 eating meals with one long-acting form of insulin per day to have a basal effect on

161
00:14:26,760 --> 00:14:32,800
 keeping glucose levels low and controlled.

162
00:14:32,800 --> 00:14:35,440
 So how about for type 2 diabetes?

163
00:14:35,440 --> 00:14:39,120
 Well, there are a plethora of different medications out there.

164
00:14:39,120 --> 00:14:44,920
 So let's go over some names and their mechanisms of action.

165
00:14:44,920 --> 00:14:55,480
 So so final ureas, also called insulin secreti-gogs, are typically taken 30 minutes before meals.

166
00:14:55,480 --> 00:15:02,360
 And their mechanism of action is to enhance insulin secretion by beta cells.

167
00:15:02,360 --> 00:15:10,380
 So we're increasing insulin secretion pretty directly.

168
00:15:10,380 --> 00:15:16,240
 And some examples of that are Glipazide, Glybyride, and Glympyride.

169
00:15:16,240 --> 00:15:23,440
 The bighuonides are taken with meals and their mechanism of action is primarily to reduce

170
00:15:23,440 --> 00:15:25,680
 glucose production.

171
00:15:25,680 --> 00:15:33,600
 And this is done by decreasing gluconeogenesis, liver production of new glucose, decreasing

172
00:15:33,600 --> 00:15:40,680
 glucose uptake from the intestines, and decreasing glucagon production, which normally moves

173
00:15:40,680 --> 00:15:44,120
 glucose into the blood.

174
00:15:44,120 --> 00:15:52,360
 So by a couple of mechanisms, the bighuonides, which are mostly metformin, is decreasing

175
00:15:52,360 --> 00:15:58,240
 the amount of glucose, reducing glucose production.

176
00:15:58,240 --> 00:16:02,040
 Next we have the dipeptidase 4 inhibitors.

177
00:16:02,040 --> 00:16:10,640
 These are the gliptins, and the gliptins are taken once daily regardless of meals.

178
00:16:10,640 --> 00:16:18,680
 And they inhibit the breakdown of the natural secreti-gogs, GLP1 and GIP, which are natural

179
00:16:18,680 --> 00:16:22,920
 hormones that stimulate insulin secretion.

180
00:16:22,920 --> 00:16:30,360
 So by inhibiting those enzymes, they indirectly enhance insulin secretion.

181
00:16:30,360 --> 00:16:42,200
 So again, I'm going to say this one is increasing insulin by inhibiting the dipeptidase 4 enzyme.

182
00:16:42,200 --> 00:16:46,320
 Next we have the thiazolidin diodes.

183
00:16:46,320 --> 00:16:48,160
 And these are glitazones.

184
00:16:48,160 --> 00:16:49,160
 These are the glitazones.

185
00:16:49,160 --> 00:16:51,680
 These are taken with meals.

186
00:16:51,680 --> 00:16:58,640
 They improve insulin sensitivity, particularly for fat and muscle cells.

187
00:16:58,640 --> 00:17:06,040
 And especially they produce more glucose transporters, so insulin will have a more profound

188
00:17:06,040 --> 00:17:10,520
 effect and suck more glucose into those cells.

189
00:17:10,520 --> 00:17:19,760
 So for this one, we can say it decreases blood glucose by increasing glucose transporters.

190
00:17:19,760 --> 00:17:26,800
 So those are often called GLUTs, glucose transporters.

191
00:17:26,800 --> 00:17:30,240
 Alright then we have the alpha-glucosidase inhibitors.

192
00:17:30,240 --> 00:17:36,440
 These are administered just before meals, and they function to delay carbohydrate digestion

193
00:17:36,440 --> 00:17:37,680
 in the gut.

194
00:17:37,680 --> 00:17:44,560
 So basically they block the breakdown of polysaccharides into glucose by this alpha-glucosidase

195
00:17:44,560 --> 00:17:46,920
 enzyme in intestinal cells.

196
00:17:46,920 --> 00:17:53,360
 And if you inhibit this enzyme, you excrete glucose instead of absorbing it.

197
00:17:53,360 --> 00:18:00,960
 However, leaving more sugars in the intestines for bacteria to ferment can cause some problems.

198
00:18:00,960 --> 00:18:07,520
 They can cause abdominal pain, nausea and diarrhea, which are common side effects from a carbose

199
00:18:07,520 --> 00:18:10,800
 and migletol.

200
00:18:10,800 --> 00:18:16,600
 So we can say for this one, you're losing glucose because you're excreting it rather

201
00:18:16,600 --> 00:18:21,000
 than absorbing it in the gut.

202
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:28,680
 And for our final row, we have the sodium glucose co-transporter to inhibitors.

203
00:18:28,680 --> 00:18:37,480
 And these are the glyph lozins, and they selectively inhibit this co-transporter, which is expressed

204
00:18:37,480 --> 00:18:43,040
 almost exclusively in the proximal convoluted tubule in the kidney.

205
00:18:43,040 --> 00:18:48,760
 So this will cause you to urinate glucose instead of reabsorbing it.

206
00:18:48,760 --> 00:18:55,760
 However, just like with the above row, leaving more sugars in the urine can feed bacteria

207
00:18:55,760 --> 00:19:01,280
 in the urethra in the bladder, causing urinary tract infections.

208
00:19:01,280 --> 00:19:07,320
 So similar to alpha-glucosidase inhibitors, this is going to decrease the amount of glucose

209
00:19:07,320 --> 00:19:12,880
 by increasing the amount that we're excreting this time in the urine.

210
00:19:12,880 --> 00:19:18,120
 Alright, so hopefully that's helpful to break down those rows and the different drugs that

211
00:19:18,120 --> 00:19:19,920
 can be used to treat diabetes.

212
00:19:19,920 --> 00:19:25,200
 I know these can appear on the board exams.

213
00:19:25,200 --> 00:19:31,000
 So there are two extremes of the blood sugar spectrum that we have to talk about.

214
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:37,400
 And they're hypoglycemia, which is low blood sugar, and hyperglycemia, which is high blood

215
00:19:37,400 --> 00:19:39,200
 sugar.

216
00:19:39,200 --> 00:19:49,960
 And someone would have hypoglycemia if they're less than 70 mg per deciliter of blood glucose.

217
00:19:49,960 --> 00:19:57,080
 And actually this is what we're most concerned about for a diabetic and the dental chair,

218
00:19:57,080 --> 00:20:01,640
 because they are then at risk for insulin shock.

219
00:20:01,640 --> 00:20:04,680
 That's too much insulin, not enough sugar.

220
00:20:04,680 --> 00:20:08,040
 The lower the blood sugar, the higher the risk.

221
00:20:08,040 --> 00:20:13,160
 And this might seem totally counterintuitive, especially from the last slide that we talked

222
00:20:13,160 --> 00:20:15,000
 about all those medications.

223
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:19,600
 Basically, everything we've talked about up to this point has been how do we reduce blood

224
00:20:19,600 --> 00:20:21,960
 sugar for diabetics.

225
00:20:21,960 --> 00:20:27,560
 But if it's reduced too much, then they can pass out via insulin shock.

226
00:20:27,560 --> 00:20:29,240
 And that's a medical emergency.

227
00:20:29,240 --> 00:20:34,160
 So there's a happy medium that we're shooting for.

228
00:20:34,160 --> 00:20:39,920
 Now if they're hypoglycemic and conscious, then we can give them orange juice or a glucose

229
00:20:39,920 --> 00:20:45,880
 tab some quick form of sugar to boost their levels and avoid insulin shock.

230
00:20:45,880 --> 00:20:50,280
 Then you can wait 15 minutes and then retest their sugar level.

231
00:20:50,280 --> 00:20:56,120
 If they're hypoglycemic and have already passed out, then it's a medical emergency we need

232
00:20:56,120 --> 00:21:03,960
 to call 911 and administer intravascular dextrose or intramuscular glucagon to help boost their

233
00:21:03,960 --> 00:21:05,880
 sugar levels.

234
00:21:05,880 --> 00:21:13,080
 Now either way here, we want to arrange for them to have an escort to drive them home.

235
00:21:13,080 --> 00:21:17,680
 So what are some signs and symptoms of hypoglycemia that we can look for?

236
00:21:17,680 --> 00:21:20,560
 Well I love this little memory tool.

237
00:21:20,560 --> 00:21:22,960
 Cold and clammy need some candy.

238
00:21:22,960 --> 00:21:27,400
 So you're feeling cold and sweaty, these are cardinal symptoms, and then getting some

239
00:21:27,400 --> 00:21:30,080
 sugary candy will help resolve them.

240
00:21:30,080 --> 00:21:37,640
 I also made up this pneumatic, tired, which stands for tachycardia, irritability, restlessness,

241
00:21:37,640 --> 00:21:41,840
 excessive hunger, and diaphoresis or sweating.

242
00:21:41,840 --> 00:21:48,760
 So a nice little card here to help you remember some of the key factors and things to remember

243
00:21:48,760 --> 00:21:51,520
 for hypoglycemia.

244
00:21:51,520 --> 00:21:55,040
 And then on the other hand, we have hyperglycemia.

245
00:21:55,040 --> 00:22:01,460
 So the specific numbers for hyperglycemia are at least 126 milligrams per deciliter

246
00:22:01,460 --> 00:22:07,400
 for fasting, at least 200 milligrams per deciliter for after a meal.

247
00:22:07,400 --> 00:22:14,080
 So the same as that red traffic light from the classification or category slide.

248
00:22:14,080 --> 00:22:16,960
 So how do you avoid or manage this?

249
00:22:16,960 --> 00:22:23,160
 Well you would promote exercise, eat less, and take insulin as directed.

250
00:22:23,160 --> 00:22:29,680
 It's not as immediately dangerous as hypoglycemia in the dental setting, but if it's chronically

251
00:22:29,680 --> 00:22:36,000
 high, it can lead to life-threatening complications like diabetic ketoacidosis that we were talking

252
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:40,720
 about before, or hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state.

253
00:22:40,720 --> 00:22:44,360
 Now how about signs and symptoms of hyperglycemia?

254
00:22:44,360 --> 00:22:49,360
 Well I also love this memory tool, hot and dry, sugar high.

255
00:22:49,360 --> 00:22:54,080
 So you feel hot and thirsty thanks to having too much sugar.

256
00:22:54,080 --> 00:22:59,680
 And I also made up this pneumatic, sinful, which stands for sweet breath, that ketone

257
00:22:59,680 --> 00:23:07,440
 breath, increased thirst, nausea and vomiting, frequent urination, polyurea, and loss of

258
00:23:07,440 --> 00:23:10,800
 clear vision or blurry vision.

259
00:23:10,800 --> 00:23:16,400
 So cold and clammy needs some candy, hot and dry, sugar high, hopefully you can remember

260
00:23:16,400 --> 00:23:23,680
 those catchy little phrases and that can help you remember stuff on the board exam.

261
00:23:23,680 --> 00:23:28,920
 Alright so here we are with our flowchart on how to manage diabetic patients.

262
00:23:28,920 --> 00:23:34,240
 And you may notice this one looks pretty similar to the hypertension one where a well controlled

263
00:23:34,240 --> 00:23:40,040
 situation has some treatment modifications while a poorly controlled situation requires

264
00:23:40,040 --> 00:23:43,920
 some bigger decisions to make.

265
00:23:43,920 --> 00:23:51,480
 So if a diabetes is well controlled, the HBA1C and the glucose are in a safe range based

266
00:23:51,480 --> 00:23:57,080
 on what we just talked about, then all routine dental procedures can be performed with only

267
00:23:57,080 --> 00:23:59,920
 a few extra considerations.

268
00:23:59,920 --> 00:24:03,240
 The short morning appointments are usually best.

269
00:24:03,240 --> 00:24:09,360
 We want to avoid aspirin and other NSAIDs in patients taking sulfonal urea drugs because

270
00:24:09,360 --> 00:24:14,320
 they can worsen hypoglycemia.

271
00:24:14,320 --> 00:24:21,680
 One of the side effects of oral glucocorticosteroids like prednisone is that they can increase blood

272
00:24:21,680 --> 00:24:29,720
 glucose levels and increase insulin resistance, making diabetes and hyperglycemia worse.

273
00:24:29,720 --> 00:24:33,200
 So avoid them when possible.

274
00:24:33,200 --> 00:24:39,600
 Levofloxasin is a potent antibiotic that can be used to treat oral infections, but it can

275
00:24:39,600 --> 00:24:45,560
 also lower blood glucose levels and worsen hypoglycemia.

276
00:24:45,560 --> 00:24:49,360
 So again avoid that if possible.

277
00:24:49,360 --> 00:24:55,000
 We want to advise the patient to take their usual insulin dose and normal meals on the

278
00:24:55,000 --> 00:25:01,160
 day of their dental appointment and confirm this with them at the appointment.

279
00:25:01,160 --> 00:25:07,080
 We want to use an office glucometer prior to dental procedures to ensure good glucose

280
00:25:07,080 --> 00:25:15,880
 control, and we're specifically trying to avoid hypoglycemia more so than hyperglycemia.

281
00:25:15,880 --> 00:25:21,320
 We want to have a glucose source ready, orange juice, soda, cake icing, and give it to the

282
00:25:21,320 --> 00:25:27,560
 patient if those cold and clammy symptoms start to occur.

283
00:25:27,560 --> 00:25:33,360
 I'd probably also add to this to be slow to change the chair position because diabetic

284
00:25:33,360 --> 00:25:38,280
 neuropathy can affect the integrity of the vasculature, and these patients may be at

285
00:25:38,280 --> 00:25:42,800
 more risk of orthostatic hypotension.

286
00:25:42,800 --> 00:25:49,480
 Now if glycemic control is poor, or if signs and symptoms point to an undiagnosed problem,

287
00:25:49,480 --> 00:25:54,560
 then there are some additional precautions that we should take.

288
00:25:54,560 --> 00:25:59,300
 If it's an elective procedure like an ascetic restoration, I would defer treatment and make

289
00:25:59,300 --> 00:26:04,000
 sure they follow up with their physician to get things back under control.

290
00:26:04,000 --> 00:26:10,800
 If it's an urgent procedure however, extracting an infected tooth, then we have to consider

291
00:26:10,800 --> 00:26:19,320
 are they asymptomatic or symptomatic for life-threatening ketoacidosis, etc.

292
00:26:19,320 --> 00:26:26,760
 And if there are no symptoms, ideally we would call the physician before proceeding if possible,

293
00:26:26,760 --> 00:26:33,240
 and then you'd want to manage any infections pretty aggressively in these patients by incision,

294
00:26:33,240 --> 00:26:40,320
 drainage, extraction, pulpotomy, and antibiotics because they have a reduced ability to fight

295
00:26:40,320 --> 00:26:45,000
 infection, and then we would refer them to their doctor.

296
00:26:45,000 --> 00:26:50,240
 If they are symptomatic, then we are immediately referring to the emergency room, get them

297
00:26:50,240 --> 00:26:56,200
 to the hospital where they could administer intravascular antibiotics, manage their insulin

298
00:26:56,200 --> 00:26:59,880
 levels appropriately, things like that.

299
00:26:59,880 --> 00:27:07,080
 One other thing to consider, brittle diabetes, which is very uncontrolled diabetes, you have

300
00:27:07,080 --> 00:27:14,680
 a higher than 200 milligrams per deciliter of fasting glucose and/or an HBA1C that's

301
00:27:14,680 --> 00:27:21,240
 greater than 9%, you might want to consider antibiotic prophylaxis before proceeding

302
00:27:21,240 --> 00:27:25,320
 as we discussed in our antibiotic prophylaxis video.

303
00:27:25,320 --> 00:27:31,320
 And again, these guidelines are a bit of a grey area, some dentists may disagree on

304
00:27:31,320 --> 00:27:36,320
 some of the things I have here and when to defer treatment, when not to, but this is

305
00:27:36,320 --> 00:27:41,000
 what I use in practice and the numbers I discussed in this video, I'm confident they're what

306
00:27:41,000 --> 00:27:44,480
 you should know for the board exam.

307
00:27:44,480 --> 00:27:50,240
 Also since there's a big correlation between diabetes and hypertension, you would only

308
00:27:50,240 --> 00:27:56,800
 follow any of the hypertension guidelines from our last video, like epinephrine restrictions,

309
00:27:56,800 --> 00:28:03,920
 only if their blood pressure indicates to do so.

310
00:28:03,920 --> 00:28:08,400
 And then there are a few oral manifestations of diabetes to be aware of.

311
00:28:08,400 --> 00:28:12,680
 Dry mouth or zerostomy is a big one and it contributes to a lot of the other ones on

312
00:28:12,680 --> 00:28:14,000
 this list.

313
00:28:14,000 --> 00:28:17,920
 Burning mouth is one of those, a burning mouth sensation.

314
00:28:17,920 --> 00:28:24,080
 A delayed wound healing is due to peripheral vascular damage, increased incidence and severity

315
00:28:24,080 --> 00:28:29,560
 of infections, this goes hand in hand with the delayed wound healing, increased risk

316
00:28:29,560 --> 00:28:36,360
 of carry thanks to dry mouth and increased glucose in the saliva, oral candidiasis also

317
00:28:36,360 --> 00:28:44,360
 thanks to dry mouth, glucose and increased infection rate, prodigland enlargement due

318
00:28:44,360 --> 00:28:50,640
 to compensatory hyperactivity of the salivary glands, try to make up for that dry mouth,

319
00:28:50,640 --> 00:28:53,280
 and then gingivitis and periodontal disease.

320
00:28:53,280 --> 00:29:01,120
 Again, zerostomyia, sugar, changes in the oral microbiome, all to blame.

321
00:29:01,120 --> 00:29:05,920
 So to finish up, I once again came up with just two scenarios here covering some frequently

322
00:29:05,920 --> 00:29:07,680
 asked questions that I get.

323
00:29:07,680 --> 00:29:13,080
 So a lot of people ask me, you know, what would you recommend for a diabetic patient who

324
00:29:13,080 --> 00:29:17,640
 is undergoing a procedure with general anesthesia?

325
00:29:17,640 --> 00:29:25,000
 Now, usually severe diabetics should not go under general anesthesia if at all possible.

326
00:29:25,000 --> 00:29:32,000
 Well-controlled diabetics can, but the typical recommendation for both IV sedation and general

327
00:29:32,000 --> 00:29:38,560
 anesthesia is fasting from midnight the night before the procedure and using only half the

328
00:29:38,560 --> 00:29:44,840
 usual insulin dose, and then supplementing with intravenous glucose during the procedure

329
00:29:44,840 --> 00:29:47,120
 as needed.

330
00:29:47,120 --> 00:29:53,040
 For the second scenario, the patient has trouble remembering if they took their insulin today.

331
00:29:53,040 --> 00:29:58,240
 Now might sound silly, but there's actually a well-established link between memory loss

332
00:29:58,240 --> 00:30:05,120
 or amnesia and diabetes, and that's because uncontrolled diabetes increases the risk of

333
00:30:05,120 --> 00:30:11,840
 cognitive problems like amnesia when you have a higher-than-normal blood glucose level

334
00:30:11,840 --> 00:30:17,000
 that can damage nerve cells and blood vessels and peripheral nerves of the body and the

335
00:30:17,000 --> 00:30:18,000
 brain.

336
00:30:18,000 --> 00:30:24,120
 Now, in terms of what you should do, it really depends on their most recent HBA1C and their

337
00:30:24,120 --> 00:30:29,000
 blood glucose level in the chair, and you can go back to our flow chart and decide how

338
00:30:29,000 --> 00:30:36,000
 to proceed based on your findings and your concerns.

339
00:30:36,000 --> 00:30:40,040
 That's it for this video guys, thank you so much for watching, please like this video

340
